---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SourceList from '../../components/SourceList.astro';
import KneeJoints from '../../components/diagrams/KneeJoints.astro';
import RangeOfMotionArc from '../../components/diagrams/RangeOfMotionArc.astro';

const refs = [
  { collection: 'sources' as const, id: 'neumann-kinesiology-2016' },
  { collection: 'sources' as const, id: 'rowe-knee-functional-rom-2000' },
  { collection: 'sources' as const, id: 'aaos-knee-arthritis' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How the knee moves"
  description="The two joints of the knee, what locking means, and how much range daily activities need."
>
  <h1>How the knee moves</h1>

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>It is two joints, not one</h2>
    <KneeJoints />
    <p>
      What we call the knee is really two joints sharing a space, and they fail in different
      ways.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>tibiofemoral joint</strong> is the one people picture: the thigh bone meeting
      the shin bone. It is the main hinge, and it carries your weight. Arthritic change and
      most of what limits bending and straightening happen here.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>patellofemoral joint</strong> is the kneecap running in a groove on the front
      of the thigh bone. The kneecap acts as a pulley, holding the quadriceps tendon away from
      the joint so the muscle has better leverage. Front-of-knee stiffness — the sort that is
      worse on stairs and after sitting — usually belongs to this joint rather than the hinge.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are worth separating because they respond to different things. Work aimed at the
      hinge does little for the kneecap, and the other way round.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>A straight knee is locked, not just straight</h2>
    <p>
      The last few degrees of straightening are not simply the end of the range. As the knee
      approaches full extension, the joint surfaces rotate slightly against each other into a
      close-packed position — the joint's most stable configuration. This is what lets you stand
      for long periods without your quadriceps working hard: the skeleton is holding you up.
    </p>
    <p>
      That lock has to be released before the knee can bend again, and{' '}
      <a href="/muscles/popliteus/">a small muscle behind the knee</a> does it by rotating the
      shin inward a few degrees.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why losing the last part of straightening matters more than the number of degrees
      suggests. A knee that will not fully straighten never reaches its locked position, so the
      quadriceps works during every second of standing. The knee feels tired as well as stiff,
      and the tiredness is real.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>How much movement do you actually need?</h2>
    <p>
      Range of motion is measured in degrees of flexion, starting from a fully straight knee at
      0°. A healthy adult knee typically bends to around 135–140°.
    </p>
    <p>
      More useful than the maximum is what everyday activities actually demand. Research
      measuring knee motion across daily tasks found:
    </p>
    <RangeOfMotionArc />
    <dl class="definition-list">
      <dt>Walking on level ground and on slopes</dt>
      <dd>Under 90° of flexion.</dd>
      <dt>Stairs, and getting in and out of chairs</dt>
      <dd>Roughly 90–120°, with lower chairs demanding more.</dd>
      <dt>Getting into a bath</dt>
      <dd>Around 135° — close to the full available range.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The same work measured an active range averaging 138° in a healthy older group, and
      suggested about 110° of flexion as a sensible goal when rehabilitating a knee — enough for
      stairs and chairs, which is most of what independence rests on.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is worth knowing because it reframes the target. The useful question is usually not
      "how do I get all my movement back" but "which activity is this stopping me doing, and how
      much would that need".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>Where the restriction actually sits</h2>
    <p>
      A knee can be short of range for several unrelated reasons, and they feel different. A
      restriction that eases as you warm up and varies day to day is behaving like muscle. One
      that arrives at the same firm point every time regardless is behaving like{' '}
      <a href="/understanding/sources/capsular-restriction/">passive tissue</a>. One that feels
      like fullness or pressure rather than tightness is behaving like{' '}
      <a href="/understanding/sources/effusion/">fluid</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      And frequently the restriction is not at the knee at all. See{' '}
      <a href="/understanding/sources/">the six sources of stiffness</a> for the rest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SourceList refs={refs} />
</BaseLayout>
